        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: خرافة",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "خرافة — khurāfa — \"a myth, a superstition\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "قال جدي إن القصة خرافة. qāla jaddī inna al-qiṣṣa khurāfa. My grandfather said the story was a myth."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Plural خرافات. Of a belief it means a superstition."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "خرافة (khurāfa) comes from a name, as Lane records under the root kh-r-f (Lane): Khurāfa, a man of the tribe of ʿUdhra, told what he had seen among the jinn and was called a liar, and khurāfāt became tales deemed pretty but untrue."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
